re  with this plot and understand the  distribution of our data well too  so for instance here if we take a look  at occupation type  and name education type two features  that are very similar  in global importance being right next to  each other uh we can see that the  occupation type  te feature uh has  most of the data just left of the 0.0  uh point on the axis of  uh marginal contributions so these most  of the data set  is slightly uh providing a marginal  contribution to a no default prediction  conversely on the name education type te  feature you can see a bubble here  just slightly to the right of 0.0 so  these  observations are providing a small  marginal contribution  towards a default prediction the other  aspect that's really great about this  plot is you can get an understanding  of outliers and how values  provide more contribution to a  prediction  such as if we look at occupation type te  the high target encoded values there  have are further towards the right side  of the x-axis  on shot value than the high name  education type  te values so those features are more  significant  uh in the contribution of  a default prediction than the high  target encoded  name education type features are  the dependence plot allows us to  understand how a single feature  affects the output of the model the  shaft dependence plots shown here  show that as x-source 2 and x-source 3  values increase the shaft values  generally decrease  as you can see here going downwards  however uh we can also see a very  interesting  shape to the shot plot for the third  most important feature  a mount annuity x and as you can see  here  the amount annuity x feature shows that  chat values can take a very non-linear  shape where the maximum shaft values on  this feature are right at about 42 000  and a feature value with a value above  42 000 or below 42  000 is going to provide a marginal  contribution that will be  less likely to default than a feature  that would have  a value around 42 000.  interactions on these features with the  most significant other feature  are also represented on these plots in  the vertical dispersion of the plot so  you can see here  that these very high contribution  features here  on amount annuity x if  if the amount goods price x is also high  that's  providing an even greater lift towards a  marginal contribution of a default  prediction  um and then you can see other values  here such as  an x source three there are some values  that have  lower chat values then other values here  and that's indicative of this  interaction with this xsource1 feature  so we've talked a lot about model  interpretability  today we'd like to then conclude our  presentation with a demonstration of how  you can  tie model interpretability with  streamline  streamlit is a user interface that  is very exciting it's uh was introduced  at pi data los angeles last december  uh stream apps are python scripts run  from top to bottom  each time a user opens your app the  script is re-executed  uh as the script executes stream draws  its output live in a browser  and there is a widget functionality that  allows the user to provide  uh simulations and you can do what-if  modeling  uh with uh streamline so  uh as you can see here in this schematic  here basically  the script is run data is stored to  cache and  the app state is outputted and rendered  in the browser  and then as the user engages with the  app  that new data then is stored to cache  and a new state in the  app is rendered in a browser so  for this project here we were really  thinking it might be interesting to see  how  can we represent uh a model  and how it changes in feature values  uh in a form that a business user could  engage with and streamlit provides the  perfect solution  so we can do what-if simulations and  there's different types of  use cases for what-if simulations of a  machine learning model  myself or chelsea us as data scientists  we can use streamlined performance  sensitivity analysis  so we can identify how our model will  perform on a range of simulated values  so that there will be no surprises when  we bring the model to production  and this is an approach that chelsea  mentioned patrick hall talked in the  past about  many of his great interpretability  techniques sensitivity analysis is just  one of them  uh and here i've just kind of extended  that sensitivity analysis and tied it in  with streamline  uh if we want to talk about business  users using streamlit  we could talk about a use case where a  finance company could have credit  analysts determine what levers could  they use  when they're reviewing an application  and identify  if they can adjust aspects of that  application  that could result in an application that  would previously be denied  to an approval state and then thirdly  operations  executives can use streamlin to  understand how changes in performance  during key touch points in a customer  experience  could result in an impact to their  likelihood to be retained  increased share of wallet and  word-of-mouth referrals that customers  may provide  as and their aspect of their experience  with the business  at key operational touch points uh  changes  so with that we'd like to now actually  move into a live demo  of streamlight so  what we're showing here is um  an app that we built in streamlin it was  very  minimal code we were able to implement  this with  under 150 lines of python code uh  streamlet provides the ability to take  user input  so here i've inputted an application  this application number of 262266  on the data set and the first data frame  is showing  the prediction that is being rendered  from the model  the probability of no default and the  probability of default  the threshold on this model is right at  0.1315 so this  this prediction is rendering a default  prediction but it's not that far off  from getting a no default prediction  below that we're then actually rendering  in a data frame each of the features  that were used in the model  the observation value that was for this  row of data  and also the shaft value so negative  shaft values are  contributing to a no default prediction  and as i scroll down here on this data  frame you can see  positive values are contributing towards  a default prediction  and then later on we can actually then  look at in a real nice plot this is an  altair plot  how do those shaft values appear plotted  out  where again blue bars indicate features  are contributing to no default  prediction  uh red bars with higher shaft values are  contributing to  a default prediction and  next then we can perform sensitivity  analysis  or what-if analysis to actually see what  happens  if we were to change some of these  values in  this row of data and how does that  impact the  prediction and the probabilities that  are outputted from our machine learning  model  so these five sliders here that i'm  rendering which happen to be the five  most important features in the model  you can see again this observation is  still showing a  a 0.17 probability of default  but as we were engaging with uh this  application one of the things that we  found that was very striking here  is this fifth feature x-source one  if we move this value just down very  slightly just one  level lower the probability of default  jumps from 0.17  to 0.22  and conversely uh as  one of the use cases that i mentioned  earlier was credit analysts may want to  use  uh this to actually see how could i  get the applicant to modify their  applications so that their application  which was in a denial or a rejection  state could  potentially change to an approval state  and  one way that we could do this is with  streamlin an analyst could actually  manipulate  some of these values so let's say amount  annuity x is the amount that the  applicant is requesting uh credit for  if we were to move this value lower and  move this down to right at about 14  000 you'll see once i just get down to  below 14 000 also now this prediction is  now  changed to a no default prediction uh i  mentioned earlier that the  threshold was right at about 42 000 for  maximum  marginal contributions this was the  feature that had that non-linear  uh hump shape and so if we move  uh this value higher you'll start to see  the probability of default decline  so if i move this to say like 60 000  that probability comes down to 0.211 and  similarly if we go  under 42 000 that probability is also  around 0.213 but as you can see as we  move that right up to about 42 000 it  starts to spike a little bit  so that gives us also a very good  understanding of how this model is  behaving  uh other features that you could see  here are the other two very important  features of x source two and x source  three  and as we manipulate these feature  values too you could see okay if this  applicant let's say these are credit  features if this applicant had better  credit which  uh is denoted by a lower value on this  uh this feature the probability of  default is excuse me  better credit is higher value worst  credit would be a lower value so if we  start moving this down to a lower value  you can see the probability default  starts to spike significantly and one of  the great things about this  uh application here is all the values  update  dynamically in real time so as i change  these values let's say  if i look at x source 3 and i move this  value from 0.7463 and you can see  currently  x source 3 is providing the most  significant  marginal contribution to a no default  prediction  if i move this value to a lower value  which is going to contribute more  towards that default prediction  this alter plot is going to update  dynamically in real time so if i move  that down to 0.4673  all of a sudden x source uh 3 is it's  still higher but the bar's starting to  come down and then if i move it even  further  now x-source three is now the fourth  most important feature  and if i move this even further down  x-source three now will be  changing colors from blue to red and now  it's contributing to a default  prediction and let's say if i was to  move this even further down let's say  down  around 0.10 it will be  close to the most important feature and  if we move it all the way down  it will now be  the most important feature contributing  to a default prediction  so this really gives us a great deal of  power in seeing how we can adjust  features with sensitivity analysis and  understanding their impact  uh through shop at the marginal  contributions to  uh the prediction that is being  outputted from the machine learning  model  other couple examples i just want to  show here is let's take an example of an  applicant  that is very likely to not default  and if i enter an application number  here you can see  this plot now showing mostly blue bars  where the feature values are  contributing to no default prediction  very few features are contributing to a  default prediction  and you can see the probability of  default is .0036  one of the aspects of the way i built  the sensitivity analysis in this plot is  that  the what if modeling here is  being populated by the mean values in  the data set so you can see if the five  most significant features  for this observation had the mean value  instead of the actual value  that probability of default increased  from .0036 to  0.0897 which is interesting still no  default prediction but it's higher  and then you can see here again if i was  to move a value let's say x source 2  to a much higher value it's going to  bring that probability of default  down and becomes the most significant  feature of no default  if i make it a much lower value it's  going to  then get a default prediction and you  can see xsource2 will be  the most significant feature towards a  default prediction  and then lastly i can show let's take a  look at an observation that is very  likely to  have a default prediction this applicant  has  out of the model 72.3 probability of  default being outputted from the model  and you can see that uh very few  features are providing marginal  contribution towards no default  most providing a marginal contribution  towards default  uh so uh there's definitely i think a  lot of  opportunity for uh streamlet uh to  really help us as data scientists have a  better and deeper understanding  of our models but also interactively  demonstrate our models to the business  as we work on our machine learning  projects  and with that that concludes my  presentation  you
 

